# Oracle probe (L11.9, ADR 0186) — what is still untrue about float *state* in the compiled
# backend (roadmap L11.6): `x /= 2` leaves x an int, and a float handed to an untyped parameter keeps
# the int word — `dbl(0.1)` answers 0 and `bump(1.5)` answers 2 where CPython answers 0.2 and 2.5,
# every one of them at exit 0 (roadmap Gap P.1, re-measured 2026-10-04 with the third shape written
# in). The two flooring lines used to be part of the divergence — `-7 // 2` compiled to -3 and
# `-3.5 % 2.0` answered -1.5, C's truncated remainder — and are now right on both backends
# (roadmap Gaps R.28, R.30, ADR 0216), so they stay here as the pair that keeps the identity
# a == (a // b) * b + (a % b) honest rather than as a report of a current defect.
print(-7 // 2)
print(-3.5 % 2.0)
x = 8
x /= 2
print(x)
def dbl(v):
    return v * 2
print(dbl(0.1))
def bump(v):
    return v + 1
print(bump(1.5))
